  "description": "Data center MEP, electrical, and structural engineering for hyperscale, colocation, and enterprise facilities. Licensed in 49 states. Built for speed.",
  "h1": "Data Center Engineering",
  "kicker": "Mission Critical · Data Center Design",
  "answer": "Apex Grid Engineering designs the mechanical, electrical, plumbing, and structural systems that keep data centers running — from hyperscale campuses to enterprise server rooms. Data center developers live and die by schedule, and our integrated MEP-plus-structural team is built to match construction speed: one coordinated package, one point of responsibility, no bureaucratic handoffs. As a veteran-owned firm licensed in 49 states with 10+ licensed professional engineers, we engineer for Tier III and Tier IV topologies, high-density AI loads, and the utility coordination that makes or breaks a project timeline.",
  "sections": [
    {"heading": "Why data center developers choose Apex Grid", "body": "Data center construction runs on a clock that traditional engineering firms were not built for. When a hyperscale developer needs permit-ready MEP drawings in weeks, not quarters, the bottleneck is almost never the design itself — it is the coordination: mechanical waiting on electrical, electrical waiting on structural, everyone waiting on the utility. Apex Grid puts MEP and structural under one roof with a single engineer of record coordinating the package. That integration is the difference between a project that hits its energization date and one that slips a quarter. We do not sell PE stamps as a standalone product; every sealed sheet comes from responsible-charge engineering."},
    {"heading": "Mission-critical MEP: power, cooling, and redundancy", "body": "Data center MEP design is the engineering of never going down. Electrical design covers utility service and substation coordination, medium-voltage distribution, UPS topology (centralized, distributed, or row-based), generator plants with paralleling and fuel storage per NFPA 110, and the power distribution chain down to the rack. Mechanical design covers the cooling architecture — air-cooled, evaporative, direct-to-chip liquid cooling, or immersion — selected for the site's climate and the rack densities the facility will actually see. Plumbing covers domestic water, fire suppression coordination, and the water systems that evaporative and liquid cooling depend on. Every system is designed to the facility's redundancy target: N, N+1, 2N, or 2(N+1)."},
    {"heading": "Structural engineering for data centers", "body": "Racks are heavy, and they keep getting heavier — AI deployments push floor loads well beyond what legacy office buildings were designed for. Structural design for data centers covers floor loading for current and future rack densities, seismic design for mission-critical facilities, roof loading for cooling equipment, and progressive collapse considerations where the risk profile demands it. Our structural and MEP teams coordinate directly, so the structural drawings already account for equipment weights, anchorage, and penetrations — no redesign loops."},
    {"heading": "From site selection to commissioning support", "body": "The cheapest data center problems are the ones caught before the land is bought. Our engineers support site due diligence: power availability and utility interconnection timelines, fiber path diversity, water supply for cooling, flood and seismic risk, and the local permitting path. During construction we support commissioning — the systematic testing (Levels 1 through 5) that proves every system performs before the first server goes live. A facility that has not been properly commissioned is a facility with unknown failure modes."}
  ],
